// Getting started: mark a machine @dev, attach an editor over the SSH facade, edit a synced workspace.
page gs_dev_attach {
  title = "Attach an editor to a dev machine"
  chapter_header "Attach an editor to a dev machine" {
    kicker       = "Getting started · tutorial"
    reading_time = "10 min read"
    updated      = "2026-09-02"
    version      = "vmlab 0.9"
  }

  p <<DOC
    A [dev machine](guide_dev) is any VM or container marked `@dev`. vmlab
    publishes it as an SSH endpoint your editor attaches into, and keeps a
    **workspace** on it, a guest-local copy of a directory on the host, in
    step with the host in both directions. In this tutorial you mark a
    container `@dev`, attach to it in one command, reach it from a plain
    `ssh`, edit files on either side and watch them arrive on the other, and
    read what the syncer is doing.
  DOC

  p <<DOC
    The lab is a trimmed version of the `dev-neovim-container` example that
    ships with vmlab. The example adds Neovim and a plugin; this tutorial
    keeps only the parts every dev machine has.
  DOC

  h2 "Before you start"

  list {
    li "vmlab is installed with the micro-VM guest asset in place, so containers boot. [Your first lab](gs_first_lab) covers the same ground."
    li "Internet access from the host, to pull `alpine:3.22`."
    li "An `ssh` client on the host. vmlab does not ship one; it writes a block into `~/.ssh/config` and hands over to yours."
    li "Optionally, an editor with remote SSH support, such as VS Code with Remote-SSH or any editor that opens files over `ssh`."
  }

  h2 "Write the lab file"

  p "In a new directory, create the lab file and the workspace directory it names."

  code sh { source = <<'SRC'
mkdir -p workspace scripts
printf 'print("hello from the workspace")\n' > workspace/hello.lua
SRC
  }

  code wcl { filename = "vmlab.wcl" source = <<'SRC'
import <vmlab.wcl>

lab "first-dev" {

  segment "lan" {
    subnet = "10.62.0.0/24"
    nat    = true
  }

  @dev(default = true, workspace = "./workspace")
  container "dev01" {
    image   = "alpine:3.22"
    profile = "container"
    cpus    = 2
    memory  = 1GiB
    mode    = :idle
    nic { segment = "lan" }

    login "dev" { user = "dev" default = true }

    provision "scripts/dev-user.ws" { }
  }
}
SRC
  }

  p "The new pieces, compared with [Your first lab](gs_first_lab)."

  list {
    li "`@dev(...)` is a decorator on the machine. `workspace = \"./workspace\"` names the host directory to sync, relative to the lab root. The guest path defaults from the profile, `/src` on Linux and `C:\\src` on Windows; set `workspace_guest` to change it. `default = true` makes this the lab's default dev machine, which matters once a lab has more than one."
    li "`mode = :idle` keeps the micro-VM up without running the image's entrypoint. A dev container has no service to be."
    li "`login \"dev\" { user = \"dev\" default = true }` declares the account every surface attaches as: `ssh`, `vmlab exec`, `vmlab shell` and the syncer itself. On a Linux machine the agent is root and needs no credential to become an account, so no password is declared. A Windows login needs one."
    li "The provision creates that account. vmlab declares logins but does not create accounts; the lab's own provisioning does, and this is the script that does it."
  }

  p "Create the provision script. It runs as the agent identity, root, which is right for installing packages and creating an account."

  code rust { filename = "scripts/dev-user.ws" source = <<'SRC'
// Create the account the lab file declares, as the machine.

use vmlab

fn sh(m: Machine, script: string, timeout: int) -> Result[string, string] {
    let r = m.exec_timeout("/bin/sh", ["-c", script], timeout)?
    if r.exit_code != 0 {
        return Err(fmt("`{}` exited {}: {}", script, r.exit_code, r.stderr))
    }
    Ok(r.stdout)
}

fn setup(lab: Lab) -> Result[unit, string] {
    let dev01 = lab.container("dev01")?
    dev01.wait_ready(600)?
    for login in dev01.logins() {
        sh(dev01, "id -u " + login.user + " >/dev/null 2>&1 || adduser -D -s /bin/sh " + login.user, 120)?
        lab.log("account " + login.user + " exists")
    }
    Ok(())
}

fn main(lab: Lab) {
    setup(lab).expect("dev01 account setup failed")
}
SRC
  }

  p <<DOC
    `dev01.logins()` reads the `login {}` declarations from the lab file, so
    the account name lives in one place. The `id -u ... ||` guard makes the
    script safe to run on every `up`.
  DOC

  code sh { source = "vmlab validate" }

  callout "The lab file validates" {
    kind = :success
    body = "`vmlab validate` reports one container. It also writes the managed block into `~/.ssh/config`: any command that loads a lab file does."
  }

  h2 "Attach in one command"

  code sh { source = "vmlab dev attach" }

  p <<DOC
    `dev attach` is cold-to-editing in one command. It brings the machine up
    if it is down, runs the provision, waits until the machine is
    **attachable**, prints the SSH alias and an editor settings snippet, and
    then becomes a shell on the machine as the `dev` login. With no argument
    it picks the machine from a fixed ladder: `VMLAB_DEV_MACHINE`, then a
    `vmlab dev use` selection, then the lab's default `@dev` machine.
  DOC

  p <<DOC
    Attachable means the agent negotiated the two features the facade needs
    for file access and port tunnels, `fileops` and `tunnel`. A container
    always has them because its agent is the one vmlab ships. On a VM built
    from an old template, `vmlab machine capabilities dev01` shows what is
    missing and `vmlab machine repair-agent` pushes the current agent in.
  DOC

  p "In the shell that opens, look at the workspace."

  code sh { source = <<'SRC'
id -un
ls /src
cat /src/hello.lua
SRC
  }

  p <<DOC
    `id -un` prints `dev`. `/src` holds `hello.lua`, placed there by the
    syncer's first pass. Leave the shell with `exit`. The machine keeps
    running and the syncer keeps running: it belongs to the lab daemon, not
    to the shell.
  DOC

  callout "You are attached" {
    kind = :success
    body = "`vmlab status` shows `dev01` ready and attachable. `dev attach` printed the alias `vmlab-first-dev-dev01`."
  }

  h2 "Reach it with plain ssh"

  p <<DOC
    The alias is a stanza in a marker-fenced block vmlab owns inside
    `~/.ssh/config`. The stanza's `ProxyCommand` is a hidden vmlab verb, so
    the system `ssh` connects through the facade with no guest network and no
    sshd in the guest. Anything that reads `~/.ssh/config` can use it.
  DOC

  code sh { source = <<'SRC'
ssh vmlab-first-dev-dev01 id -un
vmlab ssh dev01 -- uname -a
SRC
  }

  p <<DOC
    `vmlab ssh` is not a second SSH client. It refreshes the managed block and
    then hands over to the system `ssh` against the alias. Unlike `dev attach`
    it refuses if the machine is down and never starts one, the way `exec`
    and `console` behave. Copying files works the same way, because the
    facade answers `sftp` on the host.
  DOC

  code sh { source = "scp workspace/hello.lua vmlab-first-dev-dev01:/tmp/hello.lua" }

  p <<DOC
    To see the stanza and the editor snippet again, for a client that will
    not read the config file, print them.
  DOC

  code sh { source = "vmlab ssh-config --print dev01" }

  p <<DOC
    The block is written from the lab file the CLI has in hand, whenever a
    command loads a lab, and is deterministically ordered by lab, machine and
    login label. It covers declared machines, not running ones, so the alias
    stays in your editor's host list while the machine is down. A stanza is
    dropped when its lab root no longer holds a `vmlab.wcl`. vmlab refuses to
    touch the file if its markers are mangled, and names the file and line.
  DOC

  h2 "Open your editor"

  p <<DOC
    `dev attach` launches no editor and knows none. Open yours and pick
    `vmlab-first-dev-dev01` out of its SSH host list, then open `/src`. The
    snippet `dev attach` printed sets `remote.SSH.localServerDownload` to
    `always` for VS Code, so a segment without egress does not block the
    server install. For a terminal editor, run it over the alias directly.
  DOC

  code sh { source = "ssh vmlab-first-dev-dev01 -t vi /src/hello.lua" }

  h2 "Edit on either side"

  p <<DOC
    The host directory is canonical and the guest copy is a working copy.
    Edit on the host and the change lands in the guest; build in the guest
    and the output lands on the host. Try both.
  DOC

  code sh { source = <<'SRC'
printf 'print("edited on the host")\n' > workspace/hello.lua
vmlab ssh dev01 -- cat /src/hello.lua
vmlab ssh dev01 -- sh -c 'echo built > /src/out.txt'
cat workspace/out.txt
SRC
  }

  p <<DOC
    Each side has a watcher, and a changed path is applied on the other side
    after a short per-path debounce, by writing a temporary file and renaming
    it into place. Ignore rules come from the repo tree, not the lab file: a
    built-in floor, then the workspace's `.gitignore`, then a `.vmlabignore`
    for the difference, including `!` negations for gitignored files the
    guest does need, such as a `.env`. An ignored path is not skipped but
    **guest-owned**: `node_modules` is the proving case, where the guest runs
    its own install and keeps guest-native binaries there.
  DOC

  callout "Both directions work" {
    kind = :success
    body = "The host edit reached `/src/hello.lua`, and the file written in the guest appeared in `./workspace/`."
  }

  h2 "Read what the syncer is doing"

  code sh { source = "vmlab dev sync status" }

  p <<DOC
    `status` reports what the syncer last decided: paths it holds a halt on,
    warnings such as a subtree large enough to be worth naming, and the
    special files it skipped by name. `flush` runs a pass now and waits for
    it rather than for the next edit.
  DOC

  code sh { source = "vmlab dev sync flush" }

  p <<DOC
    If the same path changes on both sides between passes, the syncer
    **halts** that machine's workspace in both directions, names every path
    in the batch, writes no conflict copy, and leaves both copies where they
    were. `dev sync diff` brings the guest copy across to compare, and `dev
    sync resolve --host` or `--guest` picks a winner per path, or `--all` for
    every halted path. Making the two sides identical by hand is a third
    route that needs no verb. [Dev machines and the workspace
    syncer](guide_dev) walks through a halt.
  DOC

  callout "Snapshots are not a workspace backup" {
    kind = :warning
    body = "A snapshot restore rewinds the machine and then re-converges the guest workspace from the host copy. Work that exists only in the guest and has never reached the host is what `snapshot create` flushes first and refuses on if it cannot. Keep the host directory under version control."
  }

  h2 "Rebuild without losing the workspace"

  p <<DOC
    There is no rebuild verb because two existing verbs are one. Destroying
    the machine takes its clone and the guest working copy; the next `up`
    provisions a fresh one and the syncer seeds it again from the host.
  DOC

  code sh { source = <<'SRC'
vmlab container destroy dev01
vmlab dev attach
SRC
  }

  p <<DOC
    Everything the provision declared is back, and `/src` holds the host
    directory's current contents. Anything you installed by hand in the guest
    is gone. That is the durability rule: bake what every developer needs
    into the image or a provision, hand-install what you want today, and
    expect to redo the latter after a rebuild.
  DOC

  h2 "Clean up"

  code sh { source = <<'SRC'
vmlab down
vmlab destroy
SRC
  }

  p <<DOC
    `destroy` also forgets a `vmlab dev use` selection, which lives in the
    lab's gitignored `.vmlab/`. The `./workspace` directory on the host is
    untouched, and the alias leaves `~/.ssh/config` the next time a command
    loads a lab and finds this directory no longer has a `vmlab.wcl`.
  DOC

  h2 "Next steps"

  list {
    li "[Dev machines and the workspace syncer](guide_dev) explains the workspace model, the Windows preconditions, the halt and the snapshot bracketing in full."
    li "[Logins and the SSH facade](guide_logins) covers how a login is minted on Windows and Linux, what `elevated` means, and what the facade refuses and why."
    li "The `examples/dev-vscode-windows` example is the Windows twin: a domain member, VS Code Remote-SSH and a minted domain logon. [Example labs](apx_examples) describes both."
    li "[vmlab dev](cmd_dev), [vmlab ssh](cmd_ssh) and [vmlab ssh-config](cmd_ssh_config) document the verbs."
  }
}
